title: Rollback endpoints
tags: [[Rollback]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: The atomic POST /rollback, the plan and its summary, and the coordinated start / step / abort.

|!Route |!Does |
|`POST /repos/:repo/rollback` |navigates atomically, metadata only (no file is moved) |
|`GET /repos/:repo/rollback/plan` |the ordered steps, without the lock |
|`GET /repos/:repo/rollback/plan/summary` |counts, without the lock |
|`POST /repos/:repo/rollback/start` |takes the lock, plans, answers the first step |
|`POST /repos/:repo/rollback/step` |`{}` or `{"skip": true}`: applies the current step, answers the next |
|`POST /repos/:repo/rollback/abort` |releases the lock → `{"head"}`; HEAD stays |

''Targets'' (`{"target": …}`; for the plans, `target_id`, `target_label`,
`target_timestamp`, `target_prev_revision`):

* `{"id": N}` — operation N, anywhere in the tree;
* `{"timestamp": T}` — the most recent operation on HEAD's ancestry whose revision is no later than T;
* `{"label": L}` — the last operation of the most recent revision labelled L on HEAD's ancestry;
* `{"prev_revision": true}` — the parent of the first operation of HEAD's revision: the whole last revision undone (from the root: the empty state).

`POST /rollback` answers `{"previous_head", "new_head", "operations_unapplied",
"operations_applied"}`.

The summary — `{"total_operations", "by_type": {…}, "revisions_affected",
"filesystem_steps"}` — is what decides the form: `filesystem_steps = 0` means the
navigation rewinds in the database alone, and a client takes it in one `POST /rollback`
instead of a round-trip per operation.

!! Steps

`start` answers `{"op": {…}, "remaining": N}` — or `{"op": null, "remaining": 0}` without
taking the lock when the target is HEAD — and each `step` the next, until `op` is `null`:
the target reached, the lock released, held events applied. Each step's op carries
`filesystem`: `null`, or `{"action": "move"}` with `from`/`to` (paths to pass to `mv` as
they are; absent when the move's snapshot is not a position — the file is then
unavailable), `{"action": "restore_content"}`, `{"action": "trash_content"}`
([[Filesystem coordination]]). An inverse step also carries `entity_version_before` and
`entity_version_before_revision`, for the trash lookup (left out of forward steps, so
auto-restore never fires while re-applying a deletion).

The path is planned once, at `start`, and held with the lock, so a step costs the
operation it applies. A step finding HEAD elsewhere than the plan expects fails rather
than guess, leaving the plan for a retry or an `abort`; a second step sent while one runs
is `409`. After `abort`, reversing any move already made (or reconciling) is the client's
business.
